CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^.*symbol 'value' is unknown.*$
^.*symbol 'message_handler' is unknown.*$
--
Verifies that a sibling member-declaration following an inline
class/struct/union/enum definition whose elaboration throws is
NOT silently dropped from the enclosing class's body.

C++ standard: [class.mem]/3 — "The member-specification in a class
definition declares the full set of members of the class".  Per
[temp.inst], a member-elaboration failure must remain local to that
member; sibling members and access-specifier transitions are
independently observable parts of the member-specification.

Without the fix, the throw from elaborating `class inner : public
my_template<char>` (where `my_template<char>` fails to instantiate
because `char::nested` is undefined) abandons the body loop,
dropping the `protected:` access transition and the `value` data
member.  The method `set_value` then fails name lookup of `value`
with the spurious "symbol 'value' is unknown" diagnostic.
